# Part 6 — Up to three useful next structural experiments

These are proposed experiments only. They are **not established theorems**.

## Conjecture 1 — A core-condition version of the \(+1\) phenomenon

### Conjecture

For an appropriate class of trees \(T\) obtained by subdividing a core \(H\), perhaps under restrictions on the core and subdivision pattern,

$$
b(T)\le b(H)+1.
$$

### q=4 evidence

**[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]**

Among 2636 extremal trees:

$$
2635/2636
$$

satisfy

$$
b(T)=b(H)+1.
$$

Only one has

$$
b(T)=b(H)+2.
$$

The sole exception has core

$$
K_{1,9}.
$$

### Why worth testing

The finite dataset gives an unusually strong empirical concentration around \(+1\), while simultaneously identifying one very specific obstruction.

### Easiest counterexample

A tree whose core has very small \(b(H)\), especially

$$
b(H)=2,
$$

but whose subdivision creates several long arms.

The \(K_{1,9}\) example is exactly the first known finite candidate of this type within this dataset.

---

## Conjecture 2 — Exclude highly concentrated cores

### Conjecture

A \(+1\)-type bound may be more plausible when the core does **not** contain an extremely high-degree star-like structure such as \(K_{1,9}\).

### q=4 evidence

**[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]**

The unique \(\Delta_b=2\) tree has:

$$
\Delta(H)=9,\quad
\operatorname{diam}(H)=2,\quad
b(H)=2.
$$

No other \(b(T)=4\) tree has this core structure.

### Why worth testing

This isolates the only observed obstruction rather than treating every subdivision pattern equally.

### Easiest counterexample

Search for another tree with:

$$
b(H)=2
$$

and a highly concentrated core, followed by subdivision of several incident edges.

---

## Conjecture 3 — Number/placement of long subdivisions matters more than total subdivision

### Conjecture

For fixed

$$
\sum_e s_e,
$$

the increase in burning number depends strongly on **where the subdivision lengths are placed**, rather than merely on the total number of inserted degree-2 vertices.

### q=4 evidence

**[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]**

Every extremal tree has:

$$
\sum_e s_e=6.
$$

Yet the dataset contains:

$$
\Delta_b=1
$$

for 2635 trees and

$$
\Delta_b=2
$$

for exactly one tree.

The exceptional pattern is:

$$
(2,2,2,0,0,0,0,0,0)
$$

on \(K_{1,9}\).

### Why worth testing

It provides a natural way to separate "six inserted vertices" from their actual geometric placement.

### Easiest counterexample

Keep the same core and the same total subdivision sum, but redistribute the six subdivisions among different core edges and test whether \(\Delta_b\) changes.

